# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cador-activex-enumeration-constants-unbound — provide the vendor ActiveX constants
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-cador-activex-enumeration-constants-unbound
#+DATE: 2026-10-06

* Context

Visual LISP programs use the symbolic constants published with the AutoCAD
ActiveX API.  For example, =acAlignmentMiddleLeft= is the documented value
=9= of the =AcAlignment= enumeration.  The symbols are part of the practical
ActiveX language surface: application code should not have to replace them
with unexplained integers merely because it runs through cador.

The cador property implementation already accepts the numeric =AcAlignment=
range 0 through 14.  It does not, however, bind the corresponding AutoLISP
symbols in the runtime environment.

* Reproduction

Run the SCHME ActiveX suite with alfe's clautolisp subprocess backend:

#+begin_src sh
alfe --clautolisp --backend subprocess --host cador \
  --dialect bricscad-v26 --dwg Schema_Test.dwg \
  -l charger-dotnet.lsp -l tests/test-cad-activex.lsp \
  -x '(C:MAIN)' --quit
#+end_src

The following SCHME code creates an attribute definition and then sets its
alignment:

#+begin_src lisp
(vla-put-Alignment attribut acAlignmentMiddleLeft)
#+end_src

In GitLab job 527257, =acAlignmentMiddleLeft= evaluated to =nil= and cador
reported:

#+begin_example
ERROR [adaptateurs-activex-a1] creation insertion et mise a jour d'un bloc attribue
-- Alignment expects an acAlignment value 0-14, got NIL.
#+end_example

The remaining 16 SCHME tests passed.  This is distinct from the former
=AddAttribute= argument-order defect: attribute creation now succeeds and the
failure occurs at the subsequent =vla-put-Alignment= call.

* Cause

=clautolisp/cador/source/vlax-api.lisp= implements conversion between numeric
=AcAlignment= values and DXF groups, but the runtime bootstrap does not install
the vendor ActiveX enumeration constants.  Consequently an otherwise valid
symbolic argument reaches the property setter as =nil=.

The same gap can affect every symbolic constant used by =vla-*= and =vlax-*=
calls, not only =acAlignmentMiddleLeft=.  Fixing one name locally would leave
the ActiveX surface incomplete and make failures dependent on which application
code happens to run first.

* Temporary downstream workaround

SCHME can keep its source compatible with real CAD products by defining the
constant only when the host has not already supplied it:

#+begin_src lisp
(if (not (boundp 'acAlignmentMiddleLeft))
  (setq acAlignmentMiddleLeft 9))
#+end_src

The guard is required: AutoCAD and BricsCAD already provide this variable, so
the workaround must not overwrite a vendor definition.  The numeric fallback
is temporary and should be removed once a release containing the runtime fix
is consumed.

* Required fix

1. Inventory the ActiveX enumeration constants exposed to AutoLISP by the
   supported AutoCAD and BricsCAD profiles; do not special-case only the one
   constant found by SCHME.
2. Bind the constants in every clautolisp execution path that exposes ActiveX,
   including standalone clautolisp and =alfe --clautolisp= with direct and
   subprocess backends.
3. Keep the constant values and availability consistent with the selected host
   and product/version profile where the vendors differ.
4. Make =vl-load-com= idempotent and ensure that calling it does not erase or
   unexpectedly replace user bindings.
5. Add table-driven tests which pass symbolic constants through representative
   =vla-*= setters and methods, rather than testing only raw numeric values.

* Acceptance criteria

- Under =--host cador --dialect bricscad-v26=, =acAlignmentMiddleLeft= is bound
  to =9= before application code uses it.
- The SCHME =creation insertion et mise a jour d'un bloc attribue= test passes
  without its conditional fallback.
- All =AcAlignment= values accepted by the cador property bridge have their
  expected symbolic bindings.
- A broader generated or table-driven test covers the other ActiveX enum
  families consumed by implemented =vla-*= and =vlax-*= operations.
- Direct clautolisp, alfe direct mode and alfe subprocess mode expose the same
  bindings for the same host and dialect.
- AutoCAD/BricsCAD-compatible application code can use the symbolic constants
  without embedding numeric magic values.
